# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TEMPLATE ONLY — the live file belongs to cPanel.
#
# This is shipped as .htaccess.example and is git-IGNORED as .htaccess. The
# production repository is cloned directly into the app root, and cPanel writes
# two of its own delimited blocks into .htaccess there, rewriting them whenever
# the app is saved or restarted from the Node.js App page. Tracking the same path
# gave the file two writers, and git started refusing to merge:
#
#   error: Your local changes to the following files would be overwritten by merge:
#           .htaccess
#
# Discarding those "local changes" would have thrown away cPanel's blocks, taking
# the Passenger interpreter path and the database credentials with them.
#
# So: create the live file from this template ONCE, then leave it to cPanel. Our
# rules below sit outside cPanel's markers, so they survive its rewrites.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The marker text must match cPanel's byte for byte. cPanel finds its block by
# searching for these comments; with different wording it does not find one and
# APPENDS a second Passenger block instead, which is a duplicate-directive error.
# Note this block says "DO NOT REMOVE." while the env-vars block below it says
# "DO NOT REMOVE OR MODIFY." — that inconsistency is cPanel's, not a typo.
# DO NOT REMOVE. CLOUDLINUX PASSENGER CONFIGURATION BEGIN
PassengerAppRoot "/home/khisdsb123/sys.atline.com.my"
PassengerBaseURI "/"
PassengerNodejs "/home/khisdsb123/nodevenv/sys.atline.com.my/24/bin/node"
PassengerAppType node
PassengerStartupFile server.js
# DO NOT REMOVE. CLOUDLINUX PASSENGER CONFIGURATION END

# NOTES ON THE BLOCK ABOVE
#
# It must match cPanel's Node.js App page exactly:
#   • The home directory is /home, not /home2, on account khisdsb123. Getting
#     this wrong makes Passenger report "App root not found" and nothing else.
#   • The "/24/" is the Node version DIRECTORY under ~/nodevenv, not a version
#     string Passenger interprets. Change the version in cPanel and this line has
#     to change too, or Passenger fails with a missing-interpreter error that
#     never mentions a version number.
#
# scripts/package-production.js derives the same values for .cpanel.yml from
# CPANEL_USER / APP_DOMAIN / NODE_VERSION_DIR. If you edit this block, edit those.
#
# DELIBERATELY ABSENT: the CLOUDLINUX ENV VARS block (SetEnv DB_PASSWORD,
# JWT_SECRET and friends). cPanel maintains that on the server, and committing it
# would publish the database password and both signing secrets to GitHub.
# .env.production carries the same values, so the app has them either way — keep
# the two in step.

# NOTE ON HTTP METHODS
#
# There is deliberately no <Limit> or <LimitExcept> block here.
#
# The previous version carried two, titled "Allow HTTP methods for API" and
# "Allow DELETE method specifically". Neither did anything:
# <LimitExcept GET POST PUT PATCH DELETE OPTIONS> governs the methods NOT listed,
# so it read "allow everything else" — a no-op — and both used the Apache 2.2
# `Order allow,deny` syntax, which is a 500 on any server without
# mod_access_compat loaded. They were removed rather than left to look load-bearing.
#
# PUT, PATCH and DELETE were being refused with a 403 HTML body before Passenger
# on the previous account. Measured on this one, all three now reach the
# application and are answered in JSON, so nothing needs to be allowed here. The
# app tolerates either case regardless: the browser sends POST plus
# X-HTTP-Method-Override and scripts/method-override-hook.cjs restores the real
# method, while a genuine PUT is passed through untouched.

# Never serve the environment file, the migration SQL or the server sources as
# static content. Passenger routes everything through Node, but a
# misconfiguration that drops back to Apache must not expose credentials.
<FilesMatch "^\.env">
    Require all denied
</FilesMatch>
<FilesMatch "\.(sql|ts|tsx)$">
    Require all denied
</FilesMatch>

# This bundle is deployed by cloning the repository INTO the app root, so .git/
# sits here holding the complete history. FilesMatch cannot match a directory,
# so RedirectMatch is used instead — it can.
#
# Passenger normally hands every request to Node, which would 404 these anyway.
# The rule is here for the case where Passenger is misconfigured or stopped and
# Apache serves the directory as plain files. That is the same reasoning as the
# .env rule above, and it is the case that actually leaks.
RedirectMatch 404 ^/\.git(/|$)
